+## Subtopics
+
+### Stereo Depth-Cueing Ray Marching
+A sampling strategy that uses stereo depth priors to concentrate point sampling near tissue surfaces, replacing hierarchical stratified sampling [S3]. It discretizes ray $r(s)$ into points $\{x_j \mid x_j = r(s_j)\}$ for $j=1..m$ by sampling ray steps $s_1 \le s_2 \le \dots \le s_m$ [S3]. Steps $s_j$ are drawn from the normalized impulse distribution $\frac{1}{\xi\sqrt{2\pi}} \delta(s; u, v, i)$ [S3]. The Gaussian transfer function is formulated as $\delta(s; u, v, i) = \exp(-(s - D_i[u, v])^2 / 2\xi^2)$ [S3]. This creates an impulse distribution centered at depth $D_i[u, v]$ [S3]. $D_i[u, v]$ is the coarse depth map for frame $i$ at pixel $(u, v)$, estimated from binocular captures [S3]. $\xi$ is a hyperparameter controlling the width of the Gaussian impulse, set to a small value to mimic a Dirac delta impulse [S3].
+
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Depends on the number of input views $V$, candidates $N_{cand}$, and points $M$ or $N_{gauss}$. Calculating likelihoods requires projecting candidates to each view's depth map ($O(V \cdot N_{cand})$). Sorting and shortlisting is $O(N_{cand} \log N_{cand})$. Gaussian fitting and sampling is $O(N_{cand})$. Overall complexity is dominated by the likelihood calculation per view.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing depth maps $D^{(i)}$ and $D_{std}^{(i)}$ for all input views, and intermediate likelihood arrays for candidates.
+*   **Time Complexity:** Not given in the sources.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Not given in the sources.
 *   **Measured Performance:** Validated on the FaceScape dataset [S1]. The method allows reduction of samples per ray while maintaining or improving synthesis quality [S1, S2].
-*   **Strengths:** Focuses sampling on relevant surface regions, reducing wasted samples in empty space. Improves extrapolation capabilities beyond source view frustums when combined with source feature extrapolation techniques [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Relies on the accuracy of the depth estimator. Inaccurate depth predictions can lead to poor sampling placement.
-*   **When to use:** When depth priors are available (e.g., from monocular depth estimation or multi-view stereo) and computational efficiency is a concern.
-*   **When not to use:** When depth estimation is unreliable or unavailable, or when the scene has no clear surface structure (e.g., volumetric fog).
+*   **Strengths:** Focuses sampling on relevant surface regions, reducing wasted samples in empty space [S1, S2].
+*   **Limitations:** Relies on the accuracy of the depth estimator [S1, S2].
 
 ## Background
-The method was introduced in the DINER paper (Depth-aware Image-based NEural Radiance fields) by [S1]. It builds on pixelNeRF and leverages the TransMVSNet architecture for depth estimation [S1]. The approach exploits depth from input views that do not coincide with the target view, predicting depth at both training and test time [S1]. A related approach, NeRF-SDP, also uses depth perception for efficient generalizable NeRFs, employing a depth-encoded ray transformer and uniform sampling within confidence intervals [S2].
